    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "31ee54a6_0d611dad",
        "filename": "src/utils/span.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-13T05:02:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we can use `T*` as the iterator type because it doesn\u0027t have checked arithmetic operators. For example `dst`\u0027s `iterator+1` needs to be checked in order for\n`std::ranges::copy(src, dst.begin());`\nto be safe (this is how hardened libc++ works IIUC).\n\nIn this case, I\u0027m not sure if std::ranges::copy would be silently unsafe or if it just means it would require UNSAFE_BUFFERS, but either way that doesn\u0027t seem good. You could check this in a nocompile test, probably.\n\nThis might be a silly idea but I think you could define this as `std::span\u003cT\u003e::iterator` and define `begin()` as `return std::span(mData, mSize).begin();`. Then you don\u0027t need your own iterator type. (I did a cursory look and it seems destroying the span doesn\u0027t invalidate the iterator.)\n\nThat trick could also work for a few other things (`at`, `front`, `back`, even `first`/`last`/`subspan`) so we don\u0027t have to rely as much on the correctness of our own checks.",
      "revId": "97fcdeab515ddcf6ca779b2bb01a139b8ec4e29a",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c4846bd5_0d81a887",
        "filename": "src/utils/span.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-13T05:10:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "which is to say\n```\nauto begin() { return DAWN_UNSAFE_BUFFERS(std::span(mData, mSize)).at(i); }\nauto at(i) { return DAWN_UNSAFE_BUFFERS(std::span(mData, mSize)).at(i); }\nauto front() { return DAWN_UNSAFE_BUFFERS(std::span(mData, mSize)).front(); }\nauto first(a) { return Self(DAWN_UNSAFE_BUFFERS(std::span(mData, mSize)).first(a); }\n// etc.\n```",
      "parentUuid": "31ee54a6_0d611dad",
      "revId": "97fcdeab515ddcf6ca779b2bb01a139b8ec4e29a",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c4e43ec_3b81321d",
        "filename": "src/utils/span.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-15T15:06:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If possible, I\u0027d rather use the `DAWN_CHECK` where possible as it makes the configuration consistent in Dawn instead of having some things checked by the libc++ hardening and some by DAWN_CHECK.\n\nHowever the hardenend iterators seem pretty important. I ran into an issue with them that required adding a `\u0026*` in a test so I should have thought about it. The hack to use span\u0027s hardened iterator seems... not terrible? If possible I\u0027d like to create them directly but there might not be a portable way to do it.",
      "parentUuid": "c4846bd5_0d81a887",
      "revId": "97fcdeab515ddcf6ca779b2bb01a139b8ec4e29a",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c2e213b3_8c54f166",
        "filename": "src/utils/span.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-16T00:27:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think there\u0027s enough mixing mixing of libc++ checks and dawn checks already that it doesn\u0027t really matter. I think it\u0027s more valuable to reduce our code complexity.\n\nFor https://crbug.com/504697276 I\u0027m planning to have a single option that controls all of the checks (both dawn and libc++) so that will help unify them.",
      "parentUuid": "6c4e43ec_3b81321d",
      "revId": "97fcdeab515ddcf6ca779b2bb01a139b8ec4e29a",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a61cf1c3_a89339a7",
        "filename": "src/utils/span.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-16T14:46:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looking at Chromium\u0027s checked iterator, it doesn\u0027t do much magic, but checks that you always stay in bounds of `begin()` and `end()`. So far we\u0027re not using span iterators much so it would be nice to not add the complexity, and we can add it later if need be.",
      "parentUuid": "c2e213b3_8c54f166",
      "revId": "97fcdeab515ddcf6ca779b2bb01a139b8ec4e29a",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68a74f93_3469c343",
        "filename": "src/utils/span.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 111,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-06-17T00:15:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We\u0027re already using span iterators for `std::ranges::copy` destinations, and that needs to be safe. (Side note, I would have expected unsafe-buffers would catch if you use that with a raw pointer, though apparently [it does not](https://crbug.com/352700365).)\n\nAnd besides, if we rely on `std::span::iterator` then it barely adds any complexity.",
      "parentUuid": "a61cf1c3_a89339a7",
      "revId": "97fcdeab515ddcf6ca779b2bb01a139b8ec4e29a",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
